    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L06-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: Odd and Even Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Remember the even function \\(f\\) with this graph from earlier?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L06-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: Odd and Even Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "An equation for \\(f\\) is \\(f(x)=3x^2+1\\). Since we already know \\(f\\) is even, we also know that the output at \\(x\\) and \\(\\text-x\\) is the same for any value of \\(x\\) in the domain of \\(f\\). Said another way, \\(f(x)=f(\\text-x)\\) for all…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L06-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: Odd and Even Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For example, since \\(f(x) = 3x^2 + 1\\),",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L06-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: Odd and Even Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle \\begin{align} f(\\text-x) &= 3(\\text-x)^2 + 1 \\\\ f(\\text-x) &= 3x^2 + 1 \\\\ f(\\text-x)&= f(x) \\end{align}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L06-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: Odd and Even Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "which shows the function \\(f\\) is even.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L06-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: Odd and Even Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Let's look at a different function. Consider the function \\(g\\) defined as \\(g(x) = e^x - e^{\\text-x}\\). Using \\(\\text-x\\) as the input, we have:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L06-ACTIVITY-029",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: Odd and Even Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle \\begin{align} g(\\text-x) &= e^{\\text-x} - e^{\\text-(\\text-x)} \\\\ g(\\text-x)&= e^{\\text-x}- e^{x} \\\\ g(\\text-x)&= \\text-e^x+e^{\\text-x}\\\\ \\text-g(\\text-x)&=e^x-e^{\\text-x} \\\\ \\text-g(\\text-x)&=g(x) \\end{align}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L06-ACTIVITY-030",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: Odd and Even Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "This means \\(g\\) is odd since \\(g(x) = \\text-g(\\text-x)\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L06-ACTIVITY-031",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: Odd and Even Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A function \\(f\\) that satisfies the condition \\(f(x) = f(\\text-x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\). You can tell an even function from its graph: Its graph is symmetric about the \\(y\\)-axis.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L06-ACTIVITY-032",
      "SourceSection": "6.3: Odd and Even Equations",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A function \\(f\\) that satisfies \\(f(x) = \\text-f(\\text-x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\). You can tell an odd function from its graph: Its graph is taken to itself when you reflect it across both the \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-axes. This can also be seen as…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l06.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
